<languages />
<div class="tacos-hero">
<div class="tacos-hero-title">terraform-github-tacos</div>
<div class="tacos-hero-lead">
<translate>
<!--T:1-->
Plan OpenTofu in the pull request. Merge it, and approve the deployment to apply it.
</translate>
</div>
<div class="tacos-hero-actions">
<!-- The empty title hides the link's own title, which only repeats the label -->
<span class="tacos-button tacos-button-primary">[[Special:MyLanguage/Get started|<span title="">{{#invoke:Sequence.lua|label|Get started}}</span>]]</span>
<span class="tacos-button tacos-button-github plainlinks">[https://github.com/femiwiki/terraform-github-tacos <span>GitHub</span>]</span>
</div>
</div>

<translate>
<!--T:2-->
It does what HCP Terraform's (formerly Terraform Cloud) VCS workflow does, with GitHub Actions alone. There is no server to run, and the state stays in your own backend.

<!--T:3-->
== How it works ==
</translate>
<div class="tacos-steps">
<div class="tacos-step">
<div class="tacos-step-name">
<translate>
<!--T:4-->
Plan
</translate>
</div>
<translate>
<!--T:5-->
Every pull request plans each workspace it changes, and the plan is posted on the pull request.
</translate>
</div>
<div class="tacos-step">
<div class="tacos-step-name">
<translate>
<!--T:6-->
Merge
</translate>
</div>
<translate>
<!--T:7-->
The push to the default branch plans every workspace again, and the plan comment of the merged pull request shows the new plan.
</translate>
</div>
<div class="tacos-step">
<div class="tacos-step-name">
<translate>
<!--T:8-->
Approve
</translate>
</div>
<translate>
<!--T:9-->
The apply job waits on the workspace's GitHub environment until a required reviewer approves it. A workspace set to auto apply skips this.
</translate>
</div>
<div class="tacos-step">
<div class="tacos-step-name">
<translate>
<!--T:10-->
Apply
</translate>
</div>
<translate>
<!--T:11-->
The apply makes a fresh plan and goes ahead only when it matches the one that was approved.
</translate>
</div>
</div>

<translate>
<!--T:12-->
With <code>apply-before-merge</code>, a pull request is approved and applied first and merges once the apply succeeds, so the default branch holds only what was applied.

<!--T:13-->
== What is in it ==
</translate>
<div class="tacos-cards">
<div class="tacos-card">
<div class="tacos-card-name">
<translate>
<!--T:14-->
[[Special:MyLanguage/Runs|A GitHub Action]]
</translate>
</div>
<translate>
<!--T:15-->
Called once in each job of your workflow, with <code>step</code> naming the job.
</translate>
</div>
<div class="tacos-card">
<div class="tacos-card-name">
<translate>
<!--T:16-->
[[Special:MyLanguage/Approvals|An OpenTofu module]]
</translate>
</div>
<translate>
<!--T:17-->
Creates the environments the apply waits on, with their required reviewers.
</translate>
</div>
</div>

<translate>
<!--T:18-->
HCP Terraform also estimates what a run costs. This project leaves that to tools made for it, such as the [https://github.com/apps/infracost Infracost GitHub app], which comments the estimate on the pull request beside the plan.
</translate>
